      "key": "one Triton paged decode-attention kernel serving several batch bands at once on gfx950, where the small band is dispatch/latency-floored and the larger bands are last-level-cache-bandwidth-bound",
      "lifecycle": "active",
      "type": "lever",
      "confidence": "★★",
      "effect": "1.4781x geomean, director-verified against the frozen baseline over five rounds, per band 1.789x smallest batch / 1.571x mid / 1.149x largest; bit-exact throughout (worst-element parity PASS). The band-gating itself is what pays: the same cache-modifier edit measured 0.9974x when applied globally and became a verified gain once restricted to the bands whose KV set does not fit the mid-level cache.",
      "cost": "L2",
      "verified_on": "2026-08-12",
      "roofline": "small band stays a pure dispatch/fill floor (a few percent of CU fill, a few percent of the read roof); the larger bands move from mid-range to essentially the last-level-cache streaming rate, the largest ending ~20% above the mid band's rate on the same access pattern",
      "levers": [
        "host.launch-overhead",
        "host.launch-meta",
        "mem.cache-modifier",
        "mem.grid-map"
      ],
      "title": "Band-specialize every knob when one decode kernel serves bands in different bound classes",
      "lever": "Treat each batch band of a multi-case decode attention as its own tuning target rather than searching for one config: carry a per-band constexpr/meta table holding the cache modifier, the grid map, num_warps/num_stages/waves-per-eu and the loop-unroll factor, and let the same knob take opposite signs across bands. Pair it with a memoized direct-launch host path so the small band is not measuring the launcher.",
      "apply": "One dispatch table keyed by the band (batch-size threshold), read as constexprs inside the kernel plus a launcher-side row; the host side memoizes the compiled kernel's low-level entry with a codegenned argument builder and prunes no-op workgroups using the all-decode grid shape. Every element is value-preserving, so parity is free.",
      "stack": "total 1.4781x geomean, director-verified vs the frozen baseline = four landings compounded\n  - 1. memoized direct launcher + all-decode grid pruning - 1.302x standalone (round 1, verified) - the largest single contributor; it removes the host as the limiter on every band\n  - 2. constexpr fold of the always-true decode masks - ~+5% on the largest band only, kept at integrate for 1.3294x (round 1, verified)\n  - 3. per-band launch-meta row on the mid band only (num_warps 4 + 2-way loop unroll) - 1.3612x cumulative (round 2, verified); neither knob paid alone\n  - 4. per-band cache modifier + a locality-ordered (num_seqs, num_kv_heads) grid map, then a coherent rewrite re-tuning all three bands together with page-id prefetch - 1.4781x cumulative, ~+8.6% (rounds 3-4, verified)\n  - note: attribution is incremental in landing order, not independent - (4) re-tunes (3) rather than adding to it, and the per-band cache/grid step measured ~+3.8% against its own incoming tree but only ~+1.8% against the frozen baseline.",
      "verify": "A/B every band separately against the frozen baseline, and cross-check that the specialization ENGAGED before reading any sweep - two configs that must differ should not produce the same latency, and the compiled metadata should show the band's row, not the caller's default.",
      "pitfall": "a knob measured a net wash globally and was dropped for two rounds -> its gain on the streaming bands was cancelled by a regression on the bands whose KV set is cache-resident -> re-price it per band instead of retiring it; that revival is where the run's memory win came from.",
      "caution": "Also verify the parity gate's shape before spending a round on anything that re-associates the softmax: with the running max cast to a narrow dtype, a worst-element relative gate behaves as bit-exactness (cosine stays ~1.0 while worst-element error fails by two orders of magnitude), so the band-split family that looks free on the small band is not.",
      "source": "run kernel_20_geak_0811_2h_kb_new, 5 rounds, 2026-08-12",
